    "body": "## Problem\n\nThe §11b cross-table join in `JupyterHub/notebooks/Lakehouse_Catalog.ipynb` references column names that do not exist on `gold_transactions`. The query assumes the snake_case / `tx_*` convention used by `gold_pacs002` and `gold_pacs008` (whose ETLs rename raw fields), but `TransactionsETL` writes gold with the raw, un-renamed names.\n\nConcretely, on `gold_transactions` the columns are `endtoendid`, `tenantid`, `txsts`, `txtp`, `amt`, `ccy`, `msgid` — see [`automation-orchestrator/Table_ETLs/TransactionsETL.py` lines 167-186](https://github.com/tazama-lf/biar/blob/main/automation-orchestrator/Table_ETLs/TransactionsETL.py#L167-L186). The §11b query uses `end_to_end_id`, `tenant_id`, `tx_status`, `tx_type`, `tx_amount`, `tx_ccy` in both the `SELECT` list and the `INNER JOIN … ON` predicate against alias `t` (transactions).\n\nThis is a notebook-only bug. Every other notebook that reads `gold/transactions` (Executive_Overview, TMS_Performance, Fraud_Typology_Effectiveness, Case_Management_Trend, Anomaly_Detection_And_Rule_Calibration) applies a `_transaction_col` / `normalize_transactions_for_*` helper right after load that aliases the raw names to the underscored / `tx_`-prefixed forms, so their downstream code resolves correctly. Lakehouse Catalog registers temp views straight from `load_tenant_hudi` with no normalization step.\n\n## Logs\n\n```\nAnalysisException: [UNRESOLVED_COLUMN.WITH_SUGGESTION] A column or function parameter with name `t`.`end_to_end_id` cannot be resolved. Did you mean one of the following? [`p`.`end_to_end_id`, `t`.`endtoendid`, `p`.`orgnl_end_to_end_id`, `p`.`instd_mmb_id`, `p`.`instg_mmb_id`].; line 30 pos 36;\n'GlobalLimit 20\n+- 'LocalLimit 20\n   +- 'Sort ['t.event_ts DESC NULLS LAST], true\n      +- 'Project ['t.transaction_id, 't.end_to_end_id, 't.tenant_id, 't.tx_type, 't.tx_amount, 't.tx_ccy, 't.tx_status, 't.event_ts, 'p.dc_cdtr_acct_id AS cdtr_account_id, 'p.dc_cdtr_id AS cdtr_party_id, 'cdtr_acct.account_id AS cdtr_acct_confirmed, 'p.dc_dbtr_acct_id AS dbtr_account_id, 'p.dc_dbtr_id AS dbtr_party_id, 'dbtr_acct.account_id AS dbtr_acct_confirmed, 'p.dc_instd_amt AS dc_amount, 'p.dc_instd_ccy AS dc_ccy]\n         +- 'Join LeftOuter, (('dbtr_acct.account_id = 'p.dc_dbtr_acct_id) AND ('dbtr_acct.tenant_id = 't.tenant_id))\n            :- 'Join LeftOuter, (('cdtr_acct.account_id = 'p.dc_cdtr_acct_id) AND ('cdtr_acct.tenant_id = 't.tenant_id))\n            :  :- 'Join Inner, ((orgnl_end_to_end_id = 't.end_to_end_id) AND (tx_tenant_id = 't.tenant_id))\n```\n\nThe plan tree confirms the on-disk schema: `gold_transactions` exposes `endtoendid`, `tenantid`, `txsts`, `txtp`, `amt`, `ccy`; `gold_pacs002` exposes `end_to_end_id`, `orgnl_end_to_end_id`, `tenant_id`, `tx_status`, `tx_type`, `tx_amount`, `tx_ccy`.\n\n## Proposed solution\n\nFix the §11b cell in [`JupyterHub/notebooks/Lakehouse_Catalog.ipynb`](https://github.com/tazama-lf/biar/blob/main/JupyterHub/notebooks/Lakehouse_Catalog.ipynb) by aliasing the raw transactions columns in the `SELECT` list and using the raw names in the join predicate:\n\n- `SELECT` list — replace `t.end_to_end_id`, `t.tenant_id`, `t.tx_type`, `t.tx_amount`, `t.tx_ccy`, `t.tx_status` with `t.endtoendid AS end_to_end_id`, `t.tenantid AS tenant_id`, `t.txtp AS tx_type`, `t.amt AS tx_amount`, `t.ccy AS tx_ccy`, `t.txsts AS tx_status`.\n- Join predicate — change `p.orgnl_end_to_end_id = t.end_to_end_id AND p.tx_tenant_id = t.tenant_id` to `p.orgnl_end_to_end_id = t.endtoendid AND p.tx_tenant_id = t.tenantid`.\n- Downstream `LEFT JOIN gold_account` predicates that reference `t.tenant_id` become `t.tenantid`.\n\nScope is one cell. No ETL, table, or other notebook changes.\n\n## Impact\n\n- §11b of Lakehouse Catalog raises `AnalysisException` and cannot be run.\n- No other notebooks affected (audit confirmed the 8 other consumers of `gold/transactions` normalize column names on load).\n\n## Follow-up (out of scope for this issue)\n\nThe root inconsistency — `gold_transactions` using different column naming than `gold_pacs002`/`gold_pacs008` — should eventually be fixed in `TransactionsETL.py` so the per-notebook normalize helpers can be removed. Tracked separately.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] Lakehouse Catalog §11b executes without `AnalysisException` and returns rows joining transactions → pacs002 → account.\n- [ ] No changes outside `JupyterHub/notebooks/Lakehouse_Catalog.ipynb`.",
